so there's a lot of people who have been  talking increasingly about dual  nationale  text since 1976 Canada has affirmatively  allowed dual or multiple nationality not  just to but you can have three or four  etc other countries in the world have  also made similar moves and a lot of  countries that have more developing  economies have changed their laws in the  last 20 to 30 years to allow dual  citizenship or multiple citizenship in  part because they want to make sure that  their citizens who are now living in  other countries remain tied to their  lands of origin sometimes for social  reasons often for economic reasons they  would like those people to help  participate in the development of the  economies that they left behind people  often worry about dual citizenship that  that that participation say in the  homeland society might detract from  their participation in the country  they're living that they're way too  focused on where they came from social  science research including my own  research shows that people who are  engaged in politics in the homeland or  in issues things like human rights  violations etc in the land that they  came from actually tend to be the people  who participate even more in the  politics and the civic issues of the  place they live in and what's  interesting is things like political  skills and the sense of community and  the sense of involvement and wanting to  give back is actually something that  learns and gets layered on top of each  other
 

